A||N||1||WhiteFieroBlur||Battery trouble or what?||||8||
Z||000000||WhiteFieroBlur||05-27-2000||03:10 PM||||  I have a 1987 Fiero V6 standard. For some reason my car doesn't want to start. When I first start the car in the morning it starts up fine, then after going down the road the battery meter moves to the left side near the red and stays there. If I park the car and try to start it again it won't start up. I took the battry to get it checked but the place said that the battery was fine. A couple of months ago we had put in new brushes for the alternator and put in a new voltage regulator cause they looked fried. Sometimes the battery meter would run like its good and other times it would go down to the red. If it won't start, all I do is let the car sit for a while and try it again and it would start. But just after, I would drive it and the meter goes back down and it won't start and makes it seem like the battery is dead or dying.<BR> <BR>  Also yesterday while I was driving, I was smelling some fluid that smelled like it was rich and fresh. I am not sure if it was the fuel or something else or if it was all in my head.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR> <BR>  Also my fan belt use to break till I got a new one. Does a v6 use a V-belt or a flat belt? My fan belt is starting to squeak again but it doesn't break like the others I had. Maybe I had the wrong belt on. <P>   Can anyone help me on this? Also I just did a check on my codes and all I got was the normal code 12. I recently took off the battery to get it tested so the codes were reset. Does it take a while for the car to know whats wrong with itself before it shows the codes? Cause my car doesn't run very smooth for the computer to say its fine.||||reg||8
Z||000001||GT Bastard||05-27-2000||04:39 PM||||check the wire behind the rubber boot in the back of the alternator... I had a similar problem and this resolved it.  Under the boot, there's a wire with a metal hoop on the end that's held down by a nut. Somehow, that nut worked itself off and the wire was barely attached.  I think it's the ground wire, but I don't remember. Anyhow, it's easy to check.  Also make sure the white plug is firmly seated in the alternator.  Check and clean battery cables and make sure the OTHER end of you battery cables are clean and firmly attached.  ||||reg||1
